# How a message names the kind of a MessagePack object it did not expect: the
# name of a case of the face's `Value`, as `kind_of` answers it, in
# MessagePack's own words with their article.

# The words for the case named `kind`: "an unsigned integer", "a string",
# "nil"; a name that is none of the ten is written in backticks.
function described(kind: str) -> str
    return match kind
        "nil_value" => "nil"
        "boolean"   => "a boolean"
        "unsigned"  => "an unsigned integer"
        "negative"  => "a negative integer"
        "float"     => "a float"
        "text"      => "a string"
        "binary"    => "a binary"
        "list"      => "an array"
        "pairs"     => "a map"
        "extension" => "an extension"
        _           => f"`{kind}`"

## Tests

test "described: every case's name, and a name that is none"
    assert described("nil_value") == "nil" && described("binary") == "a binary" && described("pairs") == "a map"
    assert described("extension") == "an extension" && described("text") == "a string"
    assert described("tagged") == "`tagged`"
